<html>
<head><title>dotmatrix</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#ffffff" text="#000000" link="#0000cc">
<h2>Dot Okafor</h2>
<p>Day job: mastering engineer at a small studio in Portland. Night job: beacons.</p>
<p>I like signals that tell you where they came from. Every recording carries fingerprints of the room, the machine and the wall socket it was made with, if you know where to look.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="hum.html">Mains hum: the fingerprint of the wall socket</a></li>
<li><a href="spectro.html">Reading a spectrogram for beginners</a></li>
</ul>
</body>
</html>

@@@ home-dot-hum
type: html_page
title: Mains hum: the fingerprint of the wall socket
author: dotmatrix
date: 1999-03-20T16:00:00-08:00
path: /~dot/hum.html
role: critical
---
<html>
<head><title>Mains hum</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#ffffff" text="#000000">
<h2>Mains hum: the fingerprint of the wall socket</h2>
<p>Anything plugged into the wall in North America leaks a little 60 Hz into the recording, plus its harmonics at 120, 180 and 240 Hz. In Europe it's 50 Hz. You can't hear it most of the time. A spectrogram shows it as thin, perfectly straight lines.</p>
<p>Two things follow:</p>
<ol>
<li>If a recording claims to come from a battery radio in a field, it should not have 60 Hz lines in it.</li>
<li>If the hum appears in one part of a recording and not in another, those parts were probably not recorded on the same machine at the same time. That's a splice.</li>
</ol>
<p>Reel-to-reel decks also leave a slow wobble ("wow") around 0.5 Hz from the capstan. Cassette wobble is faster. Tape has a fingerprint too.</p>
</body>
</html>

